@node LibreClaw Usage
@section Usage

@subsection One-command entry point

If you just want to start chatting:

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-open
@end example

On first run this will:

@enumerate
@item
Ensure libreclaw is running --- auto-generating a default config
at @code{cmacs-libreclaw-default-workspace} if one is not already
configured.
@item
Detect the current project directory via @code{project-current},
@code{vc-root-dir}, or @code{locate-dominating-file} for common
markers (@file{.git}, @file{Cargo.toml}, @file{pyproject.toml},
etc.).  Falls back to the current @code{default-directory} when
no project is detected.
@item
Open a dedicated org-mode buffer named
@samp{*libreclaw: <project>*} bound to that project root.  The
first time you open a project, libreclaw starts a fresh AI
session for it.  Subsequent opens restore the conversation from
disk (@file{~/.libreclaw/cmacs-sessions/<sha>.org}) and continue
the same session.
@end enumerate

Two different projects, two different buffers, two independent
conversations.  The cmacs channel gives each project root its
own room key, so libreclaw's session manager keeps their memory
separated automatically:

@example
M-x find-file ~/Documents/notes
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-open
  -> *libreclaw: notes*     (fresh session for the notes project)

M-x find-file ~/source/projects/cmacs
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-open
  -> *libreclaw: cmacs*     (completely separate session)
@end example

Both buffers persist across Emacs restarts.

@subsection Starting and stopping manually

@code{cmacs-libreclaw-open} handles the lifecycle for you, but
you can drive it directly:

@example
(setq cmacs-libreclaw-config-file "~/.libreclaw/config.yaml")
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-start
@end example

Calling @code{cmacs-libreclaw-start} when it is already running
signals @code{cmacs-libreclaw-error}.  Use
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-running-p} to check first.

Stop with @kbd{M-x cmacs-libreclaw-stop}.  Room buffers are left in
place with their history preserved; killing them removes them from
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-rooms-alist}.

@subsection Room buffers

Each room surfaces as a buffer named
@samp{*libreclaw:CHANNEL-ID:ROOM-ID*}.  The mode is
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-room-mode}, derived from @code{org-mode}, with
a fixed structure:

@example
#+TITLE: LibreClaw — <room-name>
#+STARTUP: showall indent
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil num:nil
#+PROPERTY: channel <channel-id>
#+PROPERTY: room-id <room-id>

* Messages

** 2026-04-15 14:30:12  bob
Hey, just checking in.

** 2026-04-15 14:31:05  you
All good — working on the libreclaw integration.

* Compose                                             :compose:
<cursor parks here; type, then C-c C-c sends>
@end example

Everything above the @samp{* Compose} sentinel is read-only.  The
region below is a normal org buffer — source blocks, tables, links,
lists, footnotes all work as usual.

@subsection Sending a message

Type below @samp{* Compose} and press @kbd{C-c C-c}.  The compose
region is read, cleared, sent async through the libreclaw channel,
and inserted as a new @samp{** TIMESTAMP you} heading above the
sentinel as a local echo.

Buffer-local bindings:

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-send-compose}
@item C-c C-k
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-clear-compose}
@item C-c C-x C-f
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-attach-file}
@end table

@kbd{RET}, @kbd{TAB}, @kbd{C-c C-l} are standard org-mode keys and
work as usual for multi-line editing.

@subsection Switching between rooms

@kbd{M-x cmacs-libreclaw-show-room} prompts with a completion list
of @samp{CHANNEL/ROOM-ID} strings.

@subsection Sending programmatically

@example
(cmacs-libreclaw-send-message
 "matrix" "!roomid:example.com"
 "Hello from Elisp!")
@end example

Optional @var{HTML-BODY} and @var{THREAD-ID} arguments cover Matrix
reply threads.
